## Unnecessary Re-renders in React

### 1. Reference Equality

React re-renders a component when its props change. For primitives, change means a different value. For objects and functions, change means a different **reference** — even if the content is identical.

```js
// Every render creates a NEW object reference → child always re-renders
<Child style={{ color: 'red' }} />

// Every render creates a NEW function reference → child always re-renders
<Child onClick={() => doSomething()} />
```

JavaScript's `===` returns `false` for two distinct objects with identical contents. React uses `===` for prop diffing, so inline objects/functions always fail the equality check.

---

### 2. React.memo, useMemo, useCallback

| Tool | Memoizes | Use when |
|---|---|---|
| `React.memo` | A component's render output | Child is pure and re-renders are expensive |
| `useMemo` | A computed value | Expensive calculation whose inputs rarely change |
| `useCallback` | A function reference | Stable callback passed to a memoized child |

**`React.memo`** — wraps a component so it only re-renders when props actually change (shallow comparison):
```js
const Child = React.memo(({ onClick, label }) => <button onClick={onClick}>{label}</button>);
```

**`useCallback`** — stabilizes a function reference across renders:
```js
const handleClick = useCallback(() => doSomething(id), [id]);
```

**`useMemo`** — stabilizes an object/computed value:
```js
const style = useMemo(() => ({ color: theme.primary }), [theme.primary]);
```

> Rule of thumb: reach for these only when you have a measurable problem. Premature memoization adds complexity and has its own overhead.

---

### 3. Context Provider Pitfalls

Every consumer of a context re-renders whenever the context **value** changes. The trap: putting an inline object as the value.

```js
// BAD — new object every render → all consumers re-render every time
<UserContext.Provider value={{ user, setUser }}>
```

```js
// GOOD — stable reference, consumers only re-render when user/setUser change
const value = useMemo(() => ({ user, setUser }), [user, setUser]);
<UserContext.Provider value={value}>
```

A deeper fix: split contexts by update frequency. Put rarely-changing data (user profile) in one context and frequently-changing data (notifications count) in another so consumers only subscribe to what they need.

---

### 4. Before / After

**Before** — `ExpensiveList` re-renders on every keystroke in the parent:

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const [items, setItems] = useState([...]);

  const filtered = items.filter(i => i.active); // recalculated every render
  const handleSelect = (id) => setItems(prev => ...); // new ref every render

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <ExpensiveList items={filtered} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </>
  );
}

function ExpensiveList({ items, onSelect }) { /* renders a large list */ }
```

**After** — `ExpensiveList` is isolated from the `query` state:

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const [items, setItems] = useState([...]);

  // Stable reference — only recomputes when items change
  const filtered = useMemo(
    () => items.filter(i => i.active),
    [items]
  );

  // Stable reference — only recreated when setItems changes (never)
  const handleSelect = useCallback(
    (id) => setItems(prev => ...),
    [/* setItems is stable */]
  );

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <ExpensiveList items={filtered} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </>
  );
}

const ExpensiveList = React.memo(function ExpensiveList({ items, onSelect }) {
  /* only re-renders when filtered or handleSelect change */
});
```

Typing in the input no longer re-renders `ExpensiveList` because `filtered` and `handleSelect` both have stable references that don't change when `query` changes.
